SUMMARY:Cannupa Hanska Luger’s REFLECT — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261107T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261107T170000
DTSTAMP:20261025T162751Z
UID:ea2452d3e29aeeab1265df8ada90@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:REFLECT traces the evolution of artist Cannuap Hanska Luger’
 s Mirror Shield Project . In 2016\, when the Dakota Access Pipeline threat
 ened the waters of his ancestral homelands\, Luger circulated a video demo
 nstrating how to make “mirror shields” from inexpensive materials. Con
 ceived during the Indigenous-led resistance at Standing Rock\, North Dakot
 a\, the tutorial functioned as a call to collective action\, an invitation
  for people across the country to stand in solidarity with the Water Prote
 ctors. Carried by Water Protectors in frontline actions and collective for
 mations\, the mirrored surfaces offered protection while redirecting the f
 ield of vision. More than a thousand mirror shields were c\nCategory: Arts
  & Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cannupa-hansk
 a-lugers-reflect
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cannupa-hanska-lugers-reflect
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cracks & Reforms: The 1920s Through the GW Collection — Washingt
 on\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261107T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261107T170000
DTSTAMP:20261025T162751Z
UID:bba791110a9878fc16dab575bcd5@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:The 1920s emerged as a decade suspended between collapse and r
 econstruction\, an era marked by “cracks and reforms” that ushered in 
 a profound reconfiguration of the visual world.  During this period marke
 d by civil transformation and political upheaval\, artists\, journalists\,
  and makers had opportunities to reconstruct both individual and collectiv
 e identities. A century later\, Cracks & Reforms presents a distinct cross
 -section of this period\, revealing global currents of migration\, moderni
 zation\, and cultural redefinition through the lens of the GW Collection. 
 Students led by Associate Professor Bibiana Obler selected works to study 
 and exhibit\, their research eventually leading to unified t\nCategory: Ar
 ts & Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cracks-refo
 rms-the-1920s-through-the-gw-collection
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cracks-reforms-the-1920s-through-the-gw
 -collection
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
